    {
        "content": "<p>I'm not sure the code is even publically available so my guess is that the purpose is mainly to see what they are capable of.</p>",
        "id": 610896685,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1784136440
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If you want to see the solution(s) and/or help upstreaming to mathlib you can always contact the author(s). In <a class=\"message-link\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/583341-Model-comparisons-for-Lean/topic/LeanEval/near/609355793\">#Model comparisons for Lean &gt; LeanEval @ 💬</a> I've been able to obtain two proofs of Radó's theorem, one of them public.<br>\nAlso, a LeanEval task being solved is probably good indication that a proof can also be generated autonomously by an off-the-shelf model (or the free Aristotle), and you might try to guide a model or agent to produce a proof that meets your standards.</p>",
        "id": 610936273,
